cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "vsbench: a bias-audited virtual-screening benchmark harness"
message: "If you use this software, please cite both the software and the accompanying paper."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Parmar
    given-names: Alpesh
    email: alpeshparmar3447@gmail.com
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-11-12"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/alpeshparmar3447-ui/vsbench"
abstract: >-
  vsbench measures how much of a docking campaign's retrieval performance survives when the
  inactive compounds are matched to the actives on simple molecular properties. It draws
  three disjoint negative sets from experimentally confirmed inactives (random,
  property-matched, and DUD-E-protocol decoys), docks them with two independent scoring
  functions, selects receptor templates by redocking alone so that no benchmark label can
  influence template choice, and reports retrieval metrics alongside size-controlled
  (conditional) AUC and the performance that single molecular properties achieve on their own.
  Receptor structure quality, binding-site detection, preparation gates, docking-box strategy,
  interaction-fingerprint recovery and ADMET profiling are measured in the same run.
keywords:
  - virtual screening
  - molecular docking
  - benchmark
  - decoy bias
  - LIT-PCBA
